<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Afzal Khan (general)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1659">1659</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Afzal Khan was a leading general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shahi-dynasty">Bijapur Sultanate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-adil-shah-ii">Ali Adil Shah II</a>. In 1659 he was sent with a large army to destroy the rising power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>, and at a meeting arranged below the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pratapgad">Pratapgad</a> he was killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> leader, whose troops then routed the Bijapuri army. The encounter became one of the best-known episodes of Maratha history and tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afzal-khan-general.jpg" alt="Afzal Khan (general)" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Afzul_Khan_-_Early_20th_Century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Afzal Khan, born Abdullah Bhatari, played an important part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>'s southern expansion against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> chiefs who had taken over former Vijayanagara territory. Sent by the commander Randaula Khan to take the fort of Sira, he killed its commander Kasturi Ranga Nayaka when he came out to negotiate, and the main army then captured the fort. At the siege of Basavapattana, where Kenge Nayaka had rebelled, Afzal Khan, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahaji-bhonsle">Shahaji</a> and commanders of African origin, forced the main gate, took the qasba and beat back three counter-attacks until Kenge Nayaka surrendered.</p><p>He went on to take the fort of Chikkanayakana Halli and to besiege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belur">Belur</a>, whose chief Venkatapati became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> after a four-month siege. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahaji-bhonsle">Shahaji</a>'s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a> meanwhile began to act independently in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> region, taking lands of other Bijapur subordinates. Once Bijapur had made peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> it turned on him. The decision was probably taken by the mother of the minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-adil-shah-ii">Ali Adil Shah II</a>, who, according to a letter of the English factor Henry Revington, advised Afzal Khan to pretend friendship with Shivaji.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Afzal Khan's army numbered about 10,000 according to the English letter and the Tarikh-i-Ali; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> sources give far more, which the American academic Nicholas Gier sees as exaggeration to glorify <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>. Shiva-Bharata and other Maratha texts say he desecrated temples at Tuljapur and Pandharpur on the march, which contemporary English, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and Portuguese records do not mention. From Wai he agreed to meet Shivaji, with only a few men each, below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pratapgad">Pratapgad</a>. The Maratha texts say that Afzal Khan stabbed Shivaji during an embrace, the Mughal chronicler Khafi Khan that the treachery was Shivaji's.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>, protected by chain mail, survived; Afzal Khan was killed and beheaded, and Shivaji's hidden troops routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> army at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pratapgad">Pratapgad</a>, where, by Revington's account, around 3,000 of Afzal Khan's soldiers died. His head was buried under the Abdullah Tower at Pratapgad and his body at Javli. Marathi ballads and texts glorified the victory, likening Shivaji to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> and Afzal Khan to Ravana. From 1996 to 2004 a group of right-wing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> held Shiv Pratap Din at the fort against the annual urs at his tomb, until the state government banned the event after riots.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Afzal Khan (general)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the same time Shivaji, son of the Bijapur noble Shahaji, carved a Maratha kingdom out of Bijapur's western lands, killing the general <b>Afzal Khan</b> at Pratapgad in 1659 and defeating further expeditions sent against him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Notable campaigns under Shivaji included the capture of Torna (1646), the killing of the Bijapur general <b>Afzal Khan</b> at Pratapgad (1659) and the raid on Surat (1664).</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pratapgad">Pratapgad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17.94,73.58&amp;year=1649" aria-label="Pratapgad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6546 1498 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Afzal Khan (general)"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6996 1768h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6996 1768h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=17.94,73.58&amp;year=1649">What was here in 1649? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
